    description: "SPECULATIVE. Filed at the human's request 2026-08-11 while discussing whether\n`check-package-coverage` is worth its local wall-clock. No commitment to build;\nthis exists so the sizing and the hazards are on the record rather than\nre-derived. The human reports having the cluster materials on hand but not\nconfigured or running.\n\nTHE PROBLEM IS NOW CORE COUNT, NOT STRUCTURE, AND THAT IS MEASURED. A hypothesis\nthat the local checks were badly parallelised was REFUTED by arithmetic, so this\nitem is not a workaround for a fixable inefficiency:\n\n    gated-package serial CPU     4,795 s\n    observed wall clock            780 s   on 6 cores\n    speedup                       6.15x  = ~102% of theoretical maximum\n\nThere is no thrash to recover and no restructuring win available. Wall-clock is\nbounded by 6 cores and nothing else, which is exactly the condition under which\nadding machines is the only remaining lever.\n\nSIZING (from ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/test_timings.json, 38,391 tests tracked):\n\n    package                        tests    serial CPU-s\n    hypergumbo-core               11,720          2,573\n    hypergumbo-lang-mainstream     4,928          1,632\n    hypergumbo-tracker             2,871            361\n    hypergumbo-lang-common         1,874            219\n    extended1 + rust-analyzer      1,516             10\n    (top-level tests/)            15,478          4,561\n    TOTAL                                         9,356 s  (~2.6 CPU-hours)\n\nProjected wall at perfect scaling: 48 cores -> ~195 s; 96 cores -> ~98 s. The\nwhole suite in under two minutes is not an unreasonable target for the work\ninvolved. Both consumers benefit: `smart-test` (targeted, currently ~385 s for\n8,793 tests) and `check-package-coverage` (~780 s), and the latter is the one\nwhose cost prompted this.\n\nWHY THE WORK SUITS DISTRIBUTION: it is embarrassingly parallel at test\ngranularity, there is already a per-test timing store to bin-pack from, and\n`check-package-coverage` already writes a per-package `COVERAGE_FILE`, so the\ncombine step has a precedent in-tree rather than needing invention.\n\n*** THE HAZARD THAT SHOULD DECIDE THE DESIGN, because it is already a recorded\nlandmine rather than a speculative risk: COVERAGE IS ENVIRONMENT-DEPENDENT HERE.\n*** This repo has already been bitten by local coverage reading 100% while CI\nfailed, because this box has rust-analyzer and gitleaks installed and unrelated\ntests drove wrapper bodies that a bare environment never reaches. A HETEROGENEOUS\nCLUSTER MAKES THAT STRICTLY WORSE: the same test suite would produce different\ncoverage depending on which node drew which shard, and a 100% result would become\nnon-reproducible in a way no one could debug from the report. So node images must\nbe identical and pinned, and the design needs a check that ASSERTS identity rather\nthan assuming it — the failure mode is silent, and its symptom is a green tick.\n\nSecond-order concerns, unexamined: tests that shell out or touch /tmp (shard\nisolation); grammar build artefacts per node (currently built once and cached);\nwhether the coverage combine can distinguish \"not executed\" from \"executed on a\nnode whose report was lost\", which is the same \"a null you don't understand is not\na result\" problem this project keeps meeting.\n\nPRIOR ART TO CHECK BEFORE BUILDING ANYTHING: `pytest-xdist` already supports\nremote workers over ssh (`--tx ssh=user@host//python=python3`), so the first\nquestion is whether the existing tooling reaches far enough and a bespoke\nscheduler is unnecessary. Nobody has tried it here.\n\nRELATIONSHIP TO THE OPEN QUESTION THAT PROMPTED THIS. Separately under discussion\nis whether `check-package-coverage` should move from a local pre-push gate into\nper-PR CI. Note that relocation does NOT eliminate the wait under trunk-based\ndevelopment — it converts `local + CI` (serial) into `max(CI, pkgcov)` (parallel),\nwhich is a real saving but not to zero. A cluster is the only option that attacks\nthe wait itself. That relocation decision is independent of this item and should\nnot wait on it."  # 5550
    fields:  # 5550
      statement: "Test wall-clock should scale with available hardware rather than with one workstation's core count."  # 5550
      root_cause: "Not a defect. The local checks are already near-optimally parallel (4,795s serial completing in 780s wall on 6 cores = 6.15x, ~102% of theoretical max), so no restructuring win exists and core count is the binding constraint."  # 5550
